{"name":"Natural Cork","hex":"#976343","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"natural","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Existing in nature.","Normally associated with a particular person or thing; inherently related to the nature of a thing or creature."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A native inhabitant of a place, country, etc.","A note that is not or is no longer to be modified by an accidental."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/natural"},{"term":"cork","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The dead protective tissue between the bark and cambium in woody plants, with suberin deposits making it impervious to gasses and water.","The phellem of the cork oak, used for making bottle stoppers, flotation devices, and insulation material."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To seal or stop up, especially with a cork stopper.","To blacken (as) with a burnt cork."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cork"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cork (material)","blocks":[{"text":"Cork is an impermeable buoyant material. It is the phellem layer of bark tissue which is harvested for commercial use primarily from Quercus suber, which is native to southwest Europe and northwest Africa. Cork is composed of suberin, a hydrophobic substance. Because of its impermeable, buoyant, elastic, and fire retardant properties, it is used in a variety of products, the most common of which is wine stoppers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Cork.jpg/330px-Cork.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cork_(material)"},{"title":"Cork (city)","blocks":[{"text":"Cork is the second-largest city in the Republic of Ireland, the county town of County Cork, the largest city in the province of Munster and the third-largest on the island of Ireland. At the 2022 census, it had a population of 224,004."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/University_College_Cork_-_Quad_during_Eclipse_2015_%2816684104050%29.jpg/330px-University_College_Cork_-_Quad_during_Eclipse_2015_%2816684104050%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cork_(city)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Cork.jpg/500px-Cork.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Cork.jpg/1280px-Cork.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Cork.jpg/500px-Cork.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Untreated cork panel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACork.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Liege-algerie.jpg/500px-Liege-algerie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Liege-algerie.jpg/500px-Liege-algerie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Harvesting of cork from the forests of Algeria, 1930","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALiege-algerie.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/1-Arraiolos-0050.jpg/500px-1-Arraiolos-0050.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/1-Arraiolos-0050.jpg/500px-1-Arraiolos-0050.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Quercus suber (cork oak) bark, Portugal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1-Arraiolos-0050.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/IAPH_Saca_del_corcho.jpg/500px-IAPH_Saca_del_corcho.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.